.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
.Dd October 23, 2026
.Dt DTFL_EXECUTE_WORKER 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm dtfl_execute_worker
.Nd join an execution as one worker, on the calling thread
.Sh LIBRARY
Timely and differential dataflow library
.Pq libdtfl , \-ldtfl
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In dtfl/timely.h
.Ft int
.Fo dtfl_execute_worker
.Fa "const dtfl_config *cfg"
.Fa "size_t index"
.Fa "size_t peers"
.Fa "const dtfl_transport *transport"
.Fa "dtfl_worker_fn fn"
.Fa "void *ctx"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn dtfl_execute_worker
runs worker
.Fa index
of an execution with
.Fa peers
workers on the calling thread, and reaches every other worker through
.Fa transport .
It is for workers the host creates itself, typically one per process:
PostgreSQL backends and background workers, children of
.Xr fork 2 ,
or tasks of a runtime that has its own channels.
Each of the
.Fa peers
participants calls it once, with its own
.Fa index
and its own end of the transport, and with the same
.Fa fn ,
which must build the same dataflows in the same order on every worker,
as with
.Xr dtfl_execute 3 .
.Pp
The call runs
.Fn fn worker ctx ,
then steps the worker until its dataflows are complete, then finishes
the streams: it sends everything left and an end of stream to every
peer, and reads every peer to its end of stream.
It returns when all of that is done, so every participant returns at
about the same time.
.Pp
No thread is started.
The worker moves its own bytes: each
.Fn dtfl_worker_step
first takes what the transport has and, at its end, writes what the
step produced as far as the transport accepts it.
When the worker has nothing to do it parks in the transport's
.Fa wait ,
so arriving bytes, room on a full stream, and a
.Vt dtfl_sync_activator
fired from another thread (through the transport's
.Fa wake )
all wake it.
A worker function that waits for progress should therefore step with
.Fn dtfl_worker_step_or_park ,
which parks in the transport, rather than spin on
.Fn dtfl_worker_step .
.Pp
.Fa transport
is borrowed until the call returns and must have
.Fa send ,
.Fa recv ,
.Fa wait
and
.Fa wake ;
see
.Xr dtfl_transport 3 .
The caller frees it afterwards.
With
.Fa peers
equal to 1 it may be
.Dv NULL ,
and the worker is a single-thread worker.
.Pp
.Fa cfg
may be
.Dv NULL .
Otherwise its progress mode, log subscriptions, spill setting and idle
merge effort apply to the worker; its communication mode, thread count
and cluster addresses are ignored.
The worker's index and peer count are those passed here, and are what
.Fn dtfl_worker_index
and
.Fn dtfl_worker_peers
report.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Returns 0 once the execution is complete, or
.Dv DTFL_EINVAL
before starting anything.
A transport error during the run panics the worker with a message that
begins
.Dq timely communication error ,
as a broken cluster connection does.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Four workers in four forked processes, over a
.Xr socketpair 2
mesh made before the fork:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
int mesh[4][4];   /* mesh[i][j]: i's end of the i-j pair, -1 at i == j */
/* ... socketpair() for each i < j ... */
for (size_t i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
	if (fork() == 0) {
		dtfl_transport t;
		/* close the other workers' ends, then: */
		dtfl_transport_socket(mesh[i], 4, i, &t);
		int rc = dtfl_execute_worker(NULL, i, 4, &t, my_worker, NULL);
		t.destroy(t.ctx);
		_exit(rc != 0);
	}
}
/* the parent closes its copies and waits for the children */
.Ed
.Pp
In PostgreSQL, the leader backend creates a DSM segment holding a
.Ql shm_mq
for each ordered pair of participants, registers
.Fa peers
\- 1 dynamic background workers whose
.Va bgw_main_arg
carries the segment handle and the worker's index, and then joins as
worker 0 itself.
Every participant attaches its queues and calls
.Fn dtfl_execute_worker
with the transport shown in
.Xr dtfl_transport 3 .
Nothing in the server is threaded, so the allocation, panic and log
hooks of
.Xr dtfl_embedding 7
apply unchanged.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width DTFL_EINVAL
.It Bq Er DTFL_EINVAL
.Fa fn
is
.Dv NULL ,
.Fa index
is not below
.Fa peers ,
or
.Fa peers
is above 1 and
.Fa transport
is
.Dv NULL
or lacks
.Fa send ,
.Fa recv ,
.Fa wait
or
.Fa wake .
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dtfl_config 3 ,
.Xr dtfl_execute 3 ,
.Xr dtfl_transport 3 ,
.Xr dtfl_embedding 7
.Sh HISTORY
Upstream timely runs a process's workers as threads and starts a send
and a receive thread per remote process.
This entry runs the same allocator with those threads' work done inline
by the worker, so a process with exactly one thread can host a worker.
.Sh THREAD SAFETY
The worker belongs to the calling thread, as in
.Xr dtfl 7 .
Only the transport's
.Fa wake
is called from other threads.
